Cyclothymic Disorder
Mental Health & Substance Use
Chronic fluctuating mood, Sub-threshold hypomanic symptoms, Sub-threshold depressive symptoms

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-018
Reason for Visit: Acute severe distress and functional paralysis during a depressive swing, threatening job security.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute crisis marked by severe tearfulness, inability to leave the house for a critical work shift, and immense distress over "losing control" of their mood again. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to evaluate suicide risk during this acute low swing, provide medical certification, and stabilize the patient before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I was fine yesterday, actually feeling great and getting so much done, but today I can't even get out of bed. It never stops swinging."
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a chronic, fluctuating mood disturbance lasting over three years. During this time, there have been numerous periods with hypomanic symptoms (increased energy, talkativeness, decreased need for sleep) that do not meet the full criteria for a hypomanic episode. These are interspersed with numerous periods of depressive symptoms (lethargy, tearfulness, low motivation) that do not meet the full criteria for a major depressive episode. Symptoms have been present for at least half the time during the multi-year period, and the individual has not been without symptoms for more than two months at a time. The patient has never met criteria for a major depressive, manic, or hypomanic episode. Today's presentation is an acute sub-threshold depressive swing causing subsyndromal mood lability and immense frustration. The emotional reactivity is mild to moderate, but the rapid shifts are exhausting.
Functional Status: Currently unable to attend work due to profound sudden lethargy and tearfulness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Occupational instability due to unpredictable mood shifts; low risk of self-harm.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/76 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Fatigued, tearful but easily consolable, speaking softly but coherently.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular and respiratory systems unremarkable. Neurological exam grossly normal. Mental state examination reveals a mildly depressed affect, but without profound psychomotor retardation or active suicidal ideation. The patient provides a clear history of chronic fluctuating mood, sub-threshold hypomanic symptoms, and sub-threshold depressive symptoms.
Environmental Observations: The apartment shows signs of recent intense cleaning (from yesterday's high), juxtaposed with the patient currently unable to perform basic tasks.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular psychiatric medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cyclothymic Disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Acute depressive swing)
Differentials:

Bipolar II Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to the depressive episodes never reaching the severity or duration required for a full Major Depressive Episode.

Major Depressive Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to the frequent, distinct periods of elevated mood and energy breaking up the depressive symptoms.

Borderline Personality Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to the mood shifts being largely autonomous rather than intensely reactive to interpersonal rejection or abandonment.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute distress and functional impairment secondary to a rapid downward mood shift. Telephone triage appropriately flagged the severe tearfulness and inability to cope. Urgent after-hours medical assessment was necessary to perform a formal suicide risk assessment, differentiate a major depressive episode from a cyclothymic swing, and provide immediate occupational relief (medical certificate) to prevent job loss due to unauthorized absence. Deferring care would have exacerbated the patient's severe anxiety regarding their employment and prolonged the psychological crisis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided reassurance, validation of the chronic exhausting nature of the condition, and a medical certificate for 24 hours to alleviate the immediate occupational stress. Expected investigation results: extended daily mood diary verifying chronicity.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement "Mood Tracking." Instruct the patient to log their mood on a scale of 1-10 three times a day using a simple app. Reward one week of consistent tracking with a pre-planned, pleasant activity. This builds objective awareness of the mood cycles, replacing the feeling of being "out of control" with predictable data.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to psychology for emotional regulation strategies.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 1 week to review the mood diary and discuss potential low-dose mood stabilizer therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if thoughts of self-harm develop or if the mood rapidly elevates to extreme agitation or grandiosity.

